0/s26123801", "pmid": "42356774", "pmcid": "PMC13306937", "source": "MED", "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3390/s26123801"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306937"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306937?pdf=render"}]}}, {"title": "Evidence-based AI: from trailblazer to trustblazer?", "abstract": "Agentic AI systems can plan, call tools, and coordinate specialized sub-agents, enabling multi-step scientific workflows that exceed what single-model text generation can reliably deliver. Yet in high-stakes domains such as regulatory science and toxicology, fluent outputs are not sufficient: adoption hinges on traceability, reproducibility, context-of-use validity, and explicit uncertainty communication. This perspective argues that evidence-based medicine and evidence-based toxicology provide a mature epistemic scaffold for making agentic AI trustworthy by design. We propose an Evidence-based Agent Stack that decomposes end-to-end tasks into protocolized roles (question framing, retrieval, screening, extraction, risk-of-bias appraisal, synthesis, mechanistic/causal integration, uncertainty assessment, and evidence-to-decision translation) with mandatory provenance and versioning. Anchoring agentic workflows in systematic review practice, risk-of-bias frameworks, and emerging regulatory principles (e.g., TREAT and e-validation) can turn \"<i>trailblazing</i>\" AI into \"<i>trustblazing</i>\" AI: systems whose outputs are auditable, updateable, and aligned with decision accountability.", "doi": "10.3389/frai.2026.1818128", "pmid": "42318585", "pmcid": "PMC13272403", "source": "MED", "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1818128"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403?pdf=render"}]}}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "4c9f50a62a37671745b1138d82908a5c31768884fbfbaad0f04ffcc5352ca98c", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "judgment_and_consensus", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.3390/s26123801", "doi:10.3389/frai.2026.1818128", "pmc:PMC13306937", "pmc:PMC13272403"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.3390/s26123801", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306937", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306937?pdf=render", "https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1818128", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403?pdf=render"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

